---
id: minecraft-hot-reload
title: A Minecraft server, hot-reloaded as an agent writes it
hook: "The server is a machine like any other: each `ix apply` steps it to the new config in place, world data intact, while the agent keeps developing."
order: 7
---

A game server is the worst case for redeploys: players are connected, the
world is live state on disk, and every restart-from-scratch throws both
away. So most server code ships slowly, and nobody lets an agent near
production.

Here the Minecraft server is an ix machine declared like any other. The
agent edits the server, lands the change, and runs `ix apply`; the machine
steps to the new configuration in place. The world sits on the machine's
persistent disk and never moves. The agent keeps developing against the
running server, and the loop repeats.

